Taskferry currently has no per-provider/per-model default for reasoning effort (`--variant`). `resolveVariant` (`src/variants.js`) only handles the `"highest"` sentinel, which on `pi` always resolves to `max` and on `opencode` resolves to the top-ranked entry in that model's declared variant list. There's no way to say "this provider/model should default to `xhigh`, not `max`" — the only choices today are an explicit `--variant` on every dispatch, or `"highest"`, which is `max` in practice on `pi` regardless of model.

This matters because `max` isn't free: it costs meaningfully more reasoning tokens and latency than `xhigh` on at least one model on this account (Luna), and there's reason to suspect the same holds for DeepSeek V4's `high` vs `max`. Whether `max` earns that cost is model-specific and needs to be measured per model, not assumed — but taskferry has no config surface to act on the answer even once it's known.

## Proposal

A per-provider/per-model reasoning-effort default, structurally analogous to #499's tag registry (same `providers.<name>` config shape, same "explicit dispatch flag always overrides config default" precedence):

```jsonc
{
  "providers": {
    "meta": {
      "reasoningDefaults": {
        "muse-spark-1.2-contributor": "xhigh"
      }
    },
    "alibaba-tknplan": {
      "reasoningDefaults": {
        "deepseek-v4-pro-0813": "high"
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Resolution order: explicit `--variant` on the dispatch > this config default for the resolved provider+model > `"highest"` (today's behavior) as the fallback when no default is configured. No universal "cheap/standard/hard" bucket — every value here is an explicit level (`minimal`/`low`/`medium`/`high`/`xhigh`/`max`) tied to one provider+model pair, since supported levels and their economics differ per model.

## Relationship to #499 / #498

#499 (spec: `.superpowers/specs/2026-08-19-model-provider-tags-design.md`, #498) adds a `providers.<name>.tags` map for model *selection*. This issue is the reasoning-effort analog for model *configuration* once selected — same provider-scoped config object, different key. If #499 lands first, `reasoningDefaults` should sit alongside `tags` under the same `providers.<name>` block rather than as a separate top-level key.

Longer-term (not this issue's scope): a user-defined task tag/type — e.g. `research`, `implementation`, `review` — selecting a named profile that bundles provider + model + reasoning level in one slug, so a validated task tag resolves straight to a dispatch target instead of requiring `--provider`+`--model`+`--variant` every time. That's a third layer on top of #499's tag-to-model resolution and this issue's model-to-reasoning-default resolution; flagging it here as a followup, not proposing it as part of this change.

## Evidence this is worth doing now

Artificial Analysis shows Luna at `xhigh` scoring ~50 on their Intelligence Index off ~67M output tokens across the eval; `max`'s marginal intelligence gain over that is unverified against real taskferry workloads and worth a dedicated effort-level study before defaulting every dispatch to `max`. Filing this issue ahead of that study so the config mechanism exists once the study has an answer.
